WHAT CHANGED SINCE ROUND 2, STATED ACCURATELY

Round 2 failed the close on an empty store and said the evidence was
"permanently unavailable". I was wrong about that, and I withdraw it. The
reopen happened through the mechanism rather than around it: builder-a's
`review --fail` reopen fix (4bd08d5) moved W-021 done → building on the
recorded fail, and the twelve logs landed at 108ee23. Nothing was hand-edited;
`studio/opera/W-021.md` shows one line changed in that commit.

The method builder-b used is sound in principle and I want it on the record as
such: HEAD's test slices run against a worktree at bbddf4d, the
pre-implementation commit, through `bisellium red`. The failure is produced by
running, not transcribed. `red` refuses a command that exits 0
(lifecycle.ts:472), so no green run could have been dressed as a red. Each log
carries command, exit, clock, sella and tree. That is a real reconstruction and
it is the right instinct a second time.

It is the execution, not the method, that fails.


B1 — BLOCKING. Twelve files, three failures, and no behaviour shown failing.

  Strip each log's six header lines and the slice filename, and hash what is
  left:

    01–09  e983f12125b4b39381b9d3537698c54d   identical
    10–11  f15797255df9ced26677e49d850413de   identical
    12     f19ac6c29e4d96ffde89b51a991ea732

  Nine logs are one fact — ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND on
  `packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.js`. Two are one fact —
  ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND on `rules/paths.js`. Byte for byte, behaviour 6's red
  and behaviour 1's red are the same document with a different number typed
  into the header.

  This is the defect round 2 named in `run1-red.txt`, verbatim: "one red for
  the whole test file — it does not discriminate behaviour 1 from behaviour 12
  and shows no assertion failing." What shipped is that same artifact with
  twelve filenames on it. Splitting one failure across twelve paths satisfies
  `opus.red_evidence` — which counts files and reads `# exit:` — without
  satisfying what the files are for.

  Engineering lex §2 is the operative clause, not §7: "a behaviour is
  implemented only after its test has been run and shown failing." An
  ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND is thrown at resolve time, before a single line of the
  slice's body executes. The test did not run. Nothing was shown failing
  except the absence of a file, and the absence of a file is one fact however
  many times you record it.

