B2 — BLOCKING. The `# tree:` header on all twelve names the wrong tree.

  Every log reads `# tree: tree:b7edeb01426089311aa5ad612fefa1ffbc702f6c`.
  That is HEAD's source tree, in which `packages/cli/src/rules/evidence.ts`
  exists — 244 lines of it. Nine of those same logs say that file cannot be
  found.

  The cause is in `red`, not in builder-b's hands
  (packages/commands/src/lifecycle.ts:452-463): `sourceTreeHash(repo,
  excludeDirs, "HEAD")` hashes the repo `red` was invoked from, and
  `runCapture(cmd, process.cwd())` runs the command from the caller's cwd. When
  the command under test lives in another worktree, the header certifies a tree
  the command never touched. `red` has no way to know, and `--repo` does not
  help: it selects which repo to hash, not which tree ran.

  CLAUDE.md: "a certificate names the source tree." This one names a source
  tree that would not produce the recorded failure. It is the third false
  certificate this cascade — after round 1's and round 2's GIT_CONFIG_*
  tests-red (F4, since fixed at 2412500) — and the first one that survives in
  the officina rather than being caught and re-run.

  The practical consequence, which is why this blocks rather than files:
  nothing in the store records bbddf4d. The only trace of the tree these
  commands actually ran against is the string `w021-bbddf4d` inside a `/tmp`
  path in the `# command:` line, and that directory is gone —
  `/tmp/claude-1000/w021-bbddf4d` no longer exists. The twelve
  `__red_behaviour_N.mts` slices were never committed anywhere. A cold reader
  a cascade from now has twelve import errors certified against a tree where
  the imports resolve, and no way to reproduce or attribute any of them.

  The fix is W-020's, and small: `red` should take the tree it is certifying
  from the directory the command runs in, or record `unknown` rather than a
  hash it cannot stand behind. That is a petitio or a new opus, not an edit
  builder-b may make from here.


